---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import register from '../data/register.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const c = register.counts;

// ─── The timeline: every dated life and event the archive holds ───────────────
// lane: 'a' = the line of record · 'b' = the line the DNA points to · 's' = shared
const T0 = 1860, T1 = 2030;
const pc = (y) => ((y - T0) / (T1 - T0)) * 100;
const lives = [
  // lane, name, born, died (0 = not found), last documented year, where, events [year, kind, title, detail]
  ['a', 'Sally Leibholz & Friederike Schmul', 1847, 0, 1884, 'Hammerstein · married Szubin 1874', [
    [1874,'m','Married at Szubin','Standesamt Szubin, 1874. A Jewish family of the Posen–West Prussia borderland. Their son Jacob was born at Schubin ten years later.'],
  ]],
  ['a', 'Jacob Leibholz', 1884, 1943, 1943, 'Schubin → Berlin → Auschwitz', [
    [1924,'m','Married Auguste Luwinski','5 January 1924, Berlin — twelve years after their son Kurt was born. Kurt stayed a Luwinski; the son born after the marriage, Siegfried, was registered a Leibholz.'],
    [1943,'x','Deported to Auschwitz','26 February 1943, from Berlin. German Federal Archives Memorial Book. His brothers Samuel and Oskar died there too, and Georg at Chełmno. None of the four came back.'],
  ]],
  ['a', 'Auguste “Lizzy” Luwinski', 1893, 1975, 1975, 'Sauerbaum → Berlin → Kraichtal', [
    [1924,'m','Married Jacob Leibholz','5 January 1924, Berlin. A Mischehe: she was Catholic, from an East Prussian farming family, and she survived the war in Berlin.'],
  ]],
  ['a', 'Kurt Israel Luwinski', 1912, 1968, 1968, 'Berlin → Johannesburg, 1933', [
    [1933,'e','Left Germany','Aged 21. By 1 November 1941 he had bought the Piccadilly Hat Company at 81 Eloff Street, Johannesburg, from the widow Ivy Fuchs, with Walter Oppenheimer.'],
    [1948,'m','Married Ivone','19 June 1948, Johannesburg, certificate 858, by special licence taken out ten days before. He 36 and a bachelor; she 24 and entered DIVORCED. Both at 108 Dunottar Street, Sydenham.'],
    [1948,'d','Filed against her the same year','WLD 1950/1948 — Illiquid case, divorce, Kurt Luwinsky versus Ivone (born Mota Figueiredo). Within months of the wedding, and before the child was born.'],
    [1951,'d','Began again, and finished it','WLD 497/1951, restitution of conjugal rights — the step South African law required before a divorce for desertion — then WLD 2368/1951, the divorce itself.'],
    [1956,'m','Married Pauline Rappoport','Entry 169 of 1956. His status on the register is DIVORCEE with the order filed. The parents’ columns, which this form has and the 1948 form did not, are ruled through and empty.'],
  ]],
  ['b', 'George Castle', 1863, 1917, 1917, 'Baldock — farm, then the brewery', [
    [1883,'m','Married Caroline Wallis','1883. By the 1911 census they had been married 28 years, and her widowed father Joseph Wallis, 84, was living with them at St Mary’s Cottages, Baldock.'],
    [1917,'x','Died at Hitchin','Q3 1917, registered Hitchin, Hertfordshire, aged 54 — which confirms to the quarter what his great-granddaughter’s family tree had recorded only as “1917”.'],
  ]],
  ['b', 'Caroline Wallis', 1863, 1957, 1957, 'Weston, Hertfordshire', [
    [1883,'m','Married George Castle','1883. Seven children followed, five by 1901 and two more by 1911. She outlived him by forty years.'],
  ]],
  ['b', 'Henry Robert Wear', 1889, 1969, 1969, 'Bermondsey → Letchworth → Johannesburg', [
    [1914,'m','Married Martha Maud Castle','Hitchin, last quarter of 1914, vol 3A p.1552 — the only Wear–Castle marriage in the entire England and Wales index. By 1921 they were at 72 Pix Road, Letchworth, and he was a machine minder at J. M. Dent & Sons.'],
    [1969,'x','Died in Johannesburg','11 September 1969, aged 80. Buried at West Park Cemetery on the 13th. The Bermondsey printer’s machine minder is buried eight thousand miles from Letchworth.'],
  ]],
  ['b', 'Martha Maud Castle', 1895, 1986, 1986, 'Baldock → Letchworth → Johannesburg', [
    [1914,'m','Married Henry Robert Wear','Hitchin, Q4 1914. Six children followed, all registered at Hitchin between 1917 and 1936 — and the birth index, searched on her maiden name, is what finally listed them.'],
  ]],
  ['b', 'Leonard Aubrey Robert Wear', 1917, 1973, 1979, 'Hitchin → the army at Roberts Heights, 1940 → Johannesburg. Died 1973 or 1979 — two sources disagree, and neither is a document', [
    [1940,'e','In the army, at Roberts Heights','On his own marriage register of 7 September 1940 his occupation is entered as ARMY and his residence as ROBERTS HEIGHTS, PRETORIA — the Union Defence Force’s principal base. South Africa had entered the war one year and one day earlier. It is the first occupation recorded for him anywhere, and the first thing that explains what he was doing in South Africa at all. The clerk wrote Army and nothing else: no force, no unit, no number.'],
    [1940,'m','Married Phyllis Agnes Robertson','7 September 1940, Rosebank, Johannesburg, certificate 13. He is 23, born England, a BACHELOR, of Roberts Heights; she is 25, born Natal, a shorthand typist of 23 Derby Road, Judith Paarl. Married BY BANNS, without antenuptial contract, by W. P. Whelan — who filled in the Denomination line himself: Catholic Priest. A Catholic parish register names the parents of both parties, which no civil register of this period does. Their daughter Gillian was born eleven months later.'],
    [1948,'?','The year everything converges','He is thirty-one, in Johannesburg, and married to somebody else. The DNA says Derrick’s biological father was a son of Henry Robert Wear and Martha Maud Castle. There were two sons. The other was eleven.'],
    [1951,'d','Divorced by Phyllis','WLD 2026/1951. Six years later he was back in court against her — WLD 790/1957, by then Wear versus Phyllis Agnes NORQUOY, she having remarried in 1952. Opposed applications of that kind are usually about children.'],
    [1951,'m','Married again, that October','30 October 1951, Johannesburg, certificate 1397. He is 34, born England, a DIVORCEE, and an ENGINEER — soldier in 1940, engineer in 1951. He is living at Polana Court, Abel Road, BEREA; Ivone’s address in February 1948 was 42 Joel Road, Berea, four streets away, which is suggestive and nothing more in a suburb of tens of thousands. Marjorie Eileen Dunlop, born Walker, 34, England, divorcee, school mistress. Married on special licence 205044, WITH an antenuptial contract — the third this archive is looking for, and the only one that names Leonard.'],
    [1962,'d','Divorced again','WLD 2960/1962 — Marjorie Eileen Wear (formerly Dunlop, born Walker) versus Leonard Aubrey Robert.'],
  ]],
  ['b', 'Cynthia Yvonne Elizabeth Wear', 1931, 2000, 2000, 'Hitchin → Johannesburg', [
    [1952,'m','Married Mervin Richard Halsey','29 November 1952, St Martin’s-in-the-Veld, Johannesburg, certificate 51. She was 21 and born in ENGLAND; he was 22 and born in the CAPE PROVINCE. Two words in column four that redirected this entire archive. Her granddaughter is Derrick’s closest DNA match, at 297.5 cM. They divorced in 1978.'],
  ]],
  ['b', 'Bruce Wallace Wear', 1936, 0, 1980, 'Hitchin → Kuruman, Cape. Four marriages, three divorces. No death found', [
    [1959,'m','Married Jillian Anne Thorp','21 November 1959, Van Zyl, Kuruman, Cape Province. The younger of the two Wear sons had crossed to South Africa too.'],
    [1963,'d','Divorced','WLD 5268/1963 — Jillian Ann Wear (born Thorp) versus Bruce Wallace.'],
    [1971,'d','Divorced','WLD 6870/1971 — Rita Jean Wear (born Willis) versus Bruce Wallace Wear.'],
    [1977,'d','Divorced','WLD 489/77 — Bruce Wallace Wear versus Margaret Wear (formerly Gannon, born Crimes).'],
    [1980,'m','Married Edwina Ann Green','1980. She was 35, he was 44. She died in 2014 still called Wear and with NO SURVIVING SPOUSE named on the estate notice — which is how this archive knows he was gone before her, and all it knows.'],
  ]],
  ['b', 'Gillian Agnes Louisa Wear', 1941, 2024, 2024, 'Johannesburg → Hermanus', [
    [1969,'m','Married Willem Adrian Nel','1969. He predeceased her.'],
    [2024,'x','Died at Hermanus','17 March 2024, at 14 Fynbos Park, Church Street. Estate 10761/2024, Master of the High Court, Cape Town; identity number 4108080012088; no surviving spouse; wound up by an attorney. She had built the seven-person family tree that is the only source anywhere giving her father a mother called Castle.'],
  ]],
  ['s', 'Ivone Mota Figueiredo', 1924, 0, 1953, 'Lourenço Marques → Johannesburg → Porto. Buried at Porto, date not found', [
    [1948,'m','Married de Witt','2 February 1948, Germiston, register no. 34. She is aged 22, a dressmaker of 42 Joel Road, Berea — and entered a SPINSTER, which is what disproves the da Rocha marriage in the family tree.'],
    [1948,'m','Married Kurt Luwinski','19 June 1948 — four months and seventeen days after the first, and this time entered DIVORCED. This archive still does not understand how that was done.'],
    [1953,'m','Married Morris Isaac Salkow','13 February 1953. He died in Johannesburg in 1991 and is buried at West Park.'],
  ]],
  ['s', 'Henrique Figueiredo Pinto da Rocha', 1941, 2022, 2022, 'Born to an unmarried mother of sixteen', [
    [1968,'m','Married','18 May 1968.'],
    [2022,'x','Died in South Africa','27 October 2022. Estate 12294/2023, gazetted three times. Its death notice names his father — the man the family tree can only call “Unknown da Rocha”. That file has been on request since the beginning.'],
  ]],
];
const marks = { m:['m','Marriage'], d:['d','Divorce or action filed'], e:['e','Left the country'], x:['x','Deported, or died abroad'], '?':['q','The pivot'] };
const decades = [];
for (let y = 1860; y <= 2030; y += 20) decades.push(y);

---
<Base title="The Luwinski Archive"
  subtitle="An evidence-first archive for the family of Lynn Derrick Luwinski — Berlin, Schubin, Sauerbaum, Lourenço Marques, Johannesburg, Porto."
  lede="Four of this family's men were deported from Berlin between 1941 and 1944 and did not come back. One young man got out in 1933 and became a dress designer in Johannesburg. A woman born in Portuguese East Africa married four times in that city and was buried in Porto. This archive sets down what can be documented about all of them — and one finding that unsettles how they are joined together.">

  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card stat"><b>{c.family}</b><span>People in the family proper</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>4</b><span>In the Gedenkbuch</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>{c.index}</b><span>Surname-index entries, not descent</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>{c.omitted_living}</b><span>Living people omitted</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two spines</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={base + '/derrick/'}>Lynn Derrick Luwinski</a> is living, and this archive names him and
    says nothing else about him. Behind him run <strong>two lines, and they are not the same line.</strong>
    One is the family of record, documented to the 1870s and carried on a name. The other is the family
    his DNA keeps pointing at, reconstructed from 5,464 matches. As of <strong>15 September 2026</strong>
    that second lane has names on it all the way down, and the lean has become steep: the shared segments
    place him inside a Hertfordshire household called <strong>Wear</strong>, and a man of that family was
    living in Johannesburg in 1948. Both are still set down side by side, because <strong>no document yet
    joins the second one to him</strong> — only the arithmetic does.
  </p>

  <div class="spines">
    <div class="gen head"></div>
    <div class="node head a">
      <b>The line of record</b> <span class="tag doc">Documented</span>
      <p>Paper, from Berlin and the Posen–West Prussia borderland. The family Derrick was born into, named for, and belongs to.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="node head b">
      <b>The line the DNA points to</b> <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span>
      <p>Everyone here is documented — English registers, South African registers, a court index. What is <em>not</em> documented is the last joint, the one to Derrick.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="gen">Great-<br/>grandparents</div>
    <div class="node a">
      <span class="lane">Line of record</span>
      <b>Sally Leibholz &amp; Friederike Schmul</b>
      <p>Hammerstein; married at Szubin, 1874. A Jewish family of the borderland.</p>
      <b>Joachim Luwinski &amp; Johanna Thimm</b>
      <p>Allenstein and Sauerbaum, East Prussia. Catholic farming people of the Ermland.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="node b">
      <span class="lane">DNA line</span>
      <b>George Castle <span class="yr">b. Baldock 1863</span> &amp; Caroline Wallis <span class="yr">b. Weston 1863</span></b>
      <p>
        Married 1883. Farm labourer, later a labourer in the Baldock brewery. Hertfordshire —
        <strong>not Norfolk</strong>, an error this archive carried for weeks.
      </p>
      <b>The Bermondsey side</b>
      <p>
        <strong>Henry Robert Wear</strong>, born Bermondsey, London, 1889 — a printer&rsquo;s machine
        minder at <strong>J. M. Dent &amp; Sons</strong>, Letchworth. His own parents are not yet found.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="gen">Grandparents</div>
    <div class="node a">
      <span class="lane">Line of record</span>
      <b><a href={base + '/leibholz/'}>Jacob Leibholz</a> <span class="yr">b. Schubin 1884</span></b>
      <p>Deported from Berlin to Auschwitz, 26 February 1943. Did not come back.</p>
      <b><a href={base + '/east-prussia/'}>Auguste “Lizzy” Luwinski</a> <span class="yr">1893–1975</span></b>
      <p>Dressmaker and milliner. Survived the war in Berlin.</p>
      <div class="sibs"><span>Jacob&rsquo;s brothers</span>
        <strong>Samuel</strong> <span class="yr">1877&ndash;1943</span> &middot;
        <strong>Georg</strong> <span class="yr">1884&ndash;1942</span> &middot;
        <strong>Oskar</strong> <span class="yr">1887&ndash;1944</span> — <em>all four brothers are in the Gedenkbuch. None came back.</em>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="node b open">
      <span class="lane">DNA line</span>
      <span class="lane">DNA line</span>
      <b>Henry Robert Wear &amp; Martha Maud Castle <span class="tag inf">Named, 15 September</span></b>
      <p>
        Married at <strong>Hitchin</strong>, last quarter of <strong>1914</strong> — the only
        Wear&ndash;Castle marriage in the whole English index. By 1921 at <strong>72 Pix Road,
        Letchworth</strong>, with four children and a lodger.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>And they went too.</strong> He died in Johannesburg on <strong>11 September 1969</strong>,
        aged 80, and is buried at West Park; she died there on <strong>16 March 1986</strong>, aged 90.
        The whole household crossed.
      </p>
      <p class="verdict inf">
        <strong>The segments name them as a couple, not a line.</strong> Of the 297.5 cM Derrick shares
        with his closest match, only ~44 falls in Castle territory; <strong>~253 cM lies outside every
        region he shares with any Castle cousin</strong>. Two ancestors in common, not one.
      </p>
      <div class="sibs"><span>Martha&rsquo;s brothers and sisters</span>
        <strong>George W.</strong> 1885 &middot;
        <strong>Ada</strong> 1887 &middot;
        <strong>Frederick</strong> 1890 &middot;
        <strong>Agnes Kate</strong> <span class="yr">1891&ndash;1967</span> — <em>married Walter George Brown; the 292.7 and 333.9 cM matches both descend from her</em> &middot;
        <strong>Hilda</strong> 1902 &middot;
        <strong>Ernest</strong> 1904
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="gen">Father</div>
    <div class="node a">
      <span class="lane">Line of record</span>
      <b><a href={base + '/kurt/'}>Kurt Israel Luwinski</a> <span class="yr">b. Berlin 1912, d. Johannesburg 1968</span></b>
      <p>
        Got out of Germany in 1933, aged 21. Dress designer; partner in the Piccadilly Hat Company,
        81 Eloff Street. Married Ivone in Johannesburg, June 1948.
      </p>
      <p class="verdict disp">Very probably <strong>not</strong> the biological father: 0.0% Ashkenazi where a quarter is required, and a Y-DNA haplogroup — R-S11477, U106 — that is North Sea, not Ashkenazi.</p>
      <div class="sibs"><span>His brother</span>
        <strong>Siegfried Leibholz</strong> <span class="yr">1925&ndash;2005</span> — born after his parents married, and registered a <em>Leibholz</em> where Kurt stayed a <em>Luwinski</em>.
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="node b open">
      <span class="lane">DNA line</span>
      <span class="lane">DNA line</span>
      <b><a href={base + '/dna/#wear'}>Leonard Aubrey Robert Wear</a> <span class="yr">b. 1917</span></b>
      <p>
        Born at <strong>Hitchin</strong>, on the 1921 census at Letchworth aged four — and then absent
        from every English record there is. He was in <strong>Johannesburg</strong>, where he married
        <strong>Phyllis Agnes Robertson at Rosebank on 7 September 1940</strong>, was divorced by her in
        <strong>1951</strong>, and married again that October.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Thirty-one in June 1948</strong>, in the right city, married to somebody else. And he is
        why his sister <strong>Cynthia</strong> was in Johannesburg to marry there in 1952 — her brother
        had been there twelve years.
      </p>
      <p class="verdict inf">
        <strong>And there were only two sons.</strong> The birth index, searched on the mother&rsquo;s
        maiden name, gives the couple <strong>six children</strong> — all at Hitchin, 1917 to 1936 —
        of whom two were boys. <strong>In June 1948 one was thirty-one and the other was eleven.</strong>
      </p>
      <div class="sibs"><span>His brother and sisters</span>
        <strong>Janette E M</strong> 1919 &middot;
        <strong>Kathleen E</strong> 1921 &middot;
        <strong>Cynthia Y E</strong> 1931 — <em>married Mervin Halsey at Johannesburg in 1952; her granddaughter is the 297.5 cM match</em> &middot;
        <strong>Linda A</strong> 1933 &middot;
        <strong>Bruce Wallace</strong> 1936 — <em>married at Kuruman, Cape Province, 1959</em>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="gen">Mothers</div>
    <div class="sibrow">
      <div class="node sib span2 shared">
        <span class="rel">Mother of <em>Henrique</em> and <em>Derrick</em></span>
        <b><a href={base + '/ivone/'}>Ivone <strong>Mota</strong> Figueiredo</a></b>
        <span class="yr">b. Lourenço Marques 1924, d. Porto</span>
        <p>
          <strong>Not in dispute on either reading.</strong> Three marriages that happened — de Witt,
          Luwinski, Salkow — and a fourth in the family tree that did not. Whichever man fathered
          Derrick, she was his mother, which is also why the Portuguese half of his DNA is the half
          that behaves.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="node b open sib">
        <span class="rel">Mother of <em>Edwina</em>? <span class="tag open">If Edwina is his</span></span>
        <b>Phyllis Agnes Robertson</b>
        <span class="yr">b. 1915 &middot; married Leonard 1940, divorced him 1951</span>
        <p>
          <strong>No relation to Derrick whatsoever</strong>, and not his mother by any reading. She is
          here because <strong>Edwina&rsquo;s mother is not Ivone</strong> — if Edwina is Leonard&rsquo;s
          daughter, this is whose daughter she is, and Derrick would share a father with her and nothing
          else.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="gen">His generation</div>
    <div class="sibrow">
      <div class="node a sib">
        <span class="rel">Half-brother &middot; shared <em>mother</em></span>
        <b>Henrique Figueiredo Pinto da Rocha</b>
        <span class="yr">11 March 1941 &ndash; 27 October 2022</span>
        <p>
          Born to an <strong>unmarried mother of sixteen</strong> — the &ldquo;da Rocha marriage&rdquo;
          in the family tree never happened, and the register that disproves it enters Ivone as a
          <strong>spinster</strong> in February 1948. He carries the da Rocha name, which is the
          acknowledgement. <strong>Derrick&rsquo;s brother on either reading</strong>, because the
          dispute has only ever been about <em>his</em> father. Estate <strong>12294/2023</strong> —
          the file that would finally name the man.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="node derrick sib">
        <span class="rel">The person this archive is about</span>
        <b><a href={base + '/derrick/'}>Lynn Derrick Luwinski</a></b>
        <p>Living. Named here, and described nowhere.</p>
        <p class="verdict inf">
          <strong>Nobody on the Luwinski side.</strong> Kurt married three times and
          <strong>one child is recorded across all three</strong>. Both of the people either side of
          him here reach him through a different parent.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="node b open sib">
        <span class="rel">Half-sister &middot; shared <em>father</em> <span class="tag inf">If the reading holds</span></span>
        <b>Gillian Agnes Louisa Nel, born Wear</b>
        <span class="yr">8 August 1941 &ndash; 17 March 2024</span>
        <p>
          Born <strong>eleven months</strong> after Leonard married <strong>Phyllis Agnes</strong>
          Robertson, and carrying her mother&rsquo;s name. Married <strong>Willem Adrian Nel</strong> in
          <strong>1969</strong>; he predeceased her. She died at <strong>14 Fynbos Park, Church Street,
          Hermanus</strong> — estate <strong>10761/2024</strong>, identity number 4108080012088, no
          surviving spouse, wound up by an attorney rather than by family.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>She built the tree.</strong> Its own page labels Leonard <em>&ldquo;Father (of site
          manager)&rdquo;</em>, which is how the <em>&lt;Private&gt;</em> daughter is identified — and
          it is the only source anywhere that gives Leonard a mother called <strong>Castle</strong>.
          <strong>She died in March 2024</strong>, so the half-sibling test that would have ended this
          in a single reading is gone.
        </p>
        <p class="verdict inf">
          Three more women born <strong>Wear</strong> inside that marriage, all dead, all with estates
          now found free in the gazette: <strong>Doris Dawn Joubert</strong> 1943, <strong>Patricia Mary
          Maud Grant</strong> 1946 (<em>018561/2021</em>, and she too died in the Cape), <strong>Heather
          Jill Hunter</strong> 1949. Her sisters, or another family altogether.
          <strong>Two death notices were requested from the Master at Cape Town on 15 September</strong>
          — each has a field for the father&rsquo;s name.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="node b open sib" style="grid-column: 1 / span 3;">
        <span class="rel">And the woman who stood in this row this morning <span class="tag disp">Removed &mdash; she was nobody</span></span>
        <b>Edwina Ann Wear</b>
        <span class="yr">28 May 1945 &ndash; 30 June 2014</span>
        <p>
          She was a Wear <strong>by marriage</strong>. Born <strong>Edwina Ann GREEN</strong>, she
          married <strong>Bruce Wallace Wear</strong> — <em>Leonard&rsquo;s younger brother</em>, the boy
          who was eleven in 1948 — in <strong>1980</strong>. <strong>No blood relation to Derrick at
          all.</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="verdict disp">
          Every dead end around her was the answer, telling us so. Her parents were unfindable under
          Wear because they were Greens; she was still a Wear at 69 because it was her married name;
          the estate notice named no surviving spouse because Bruce was already gone. This archive
          called her &ldquo;a coincidence with good timing&rdquo; for a day. She was a coincidence.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="gen"></div>
    <div class="note both">
      <strong>What would turn the right-hand lane from a strong guess into a fact:</strong>
      <strong>WLD 2026/1951</strong> and <strong>WLD 790/1957</strong> in the National Archives at
      Pretoria — a divorce, and six years later an opposed application against the same woman, which in
      that court usually means children. Or a living male-line Wear taking a Y test, whose result should
      be Derrick&rsquo;s. <a href={base + '/dna/#wear'}>How the segments got here, and what is still
      only inferred &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </div>


  <h2>Everything dated, on one axis</h2>
  <p>
    The two spines say who stands where. <strong>This says when.</strong> Every life the archive can
    date, drawn to the same scale — the Berlin line above, the Hertfordshire line below, and the two
    people both readings share between them. <strong>Derrick is not on it</strong>, because he is living
    and this archive gives him no dates at all.
  </p>

  <div class="scroll"><div class="tl">
    <div class="tl-axis">
      {decades.map(y => (
        <div class="tl-tick" style={`left:${pc(y)}%`}><span>{y}</span></div>
      ))}
    </div>
    {lives.map(([lane, name, b, d, last, where, evs]) => (
      <div class={'tl-row ' + lane}>
        <div class="tl-name"><b>{name}</b><span>{where}</span></div>
        <div class="tl-track">
          {decades.map(y => (<div class="tl-grid" style={`left:${pc(y)}%`}></div>))}
          <div class="tl-bar" style={`left:${pc(b)}%; width:${pc(d || last) - pc(b)}%`}>
            <span class="tl-b">{b}</span>
            {d ? <span class="tl-d">{d}</span> : null}
          </div>
          {d ? null : (
            <div class="tl-tail" style={`left:${pc(last)}%; width:${pc(last + 14) - pc(last)}%`}>
              <span class="tl-d open">?</span>
            </div>
          )}
          {evs.map(([y, k, title, detail]) => (
            <button class={'tl-ev ' + (k === '?' ? 'q' : k)} style={`left:${pc(y)}%`}
              data-y={y} data-who={name} data-kind={marks[k][1]} data-title={title} data-detail={detail}
              aria-label={`${y} — ${title}`}></button>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div></div>

  <div class="tl-detail" id="tlDetail" hidden>
    <button class="tl-close" id="tlClose" aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
    <span class="tl-who" id="tlWho"></span>
    <b id="tlTitle"></b>
    <span class="tl-meta" id="tlMeta"></span>
    <p id="tlText"></p>
  </div>
  <p class="small tl-hint" id="tlHint">
    <strong>Every dot on the chart is clickable.</strong> Forty-odd events — marriages, divorces,
    deportations, departures — each with what the archive actually holds on it.
  </p>

  <p class="small">
    <span class="tl-key"><i class="m"></i> marriage</span>
    <span class="tl-key"><i class="d"></i> divorce or action filed</span>
    <span class="tl-key"><i class="e"></i> left the country</span>
    <span class="tl-key"><i class="x"></i> deported, or died abroad</span>
    <span class="tl-key"><i class="q"></i> the pivot</span>
    <span class="tl-key">a <strong>dotted tail</strong> is a life this archive cannot see the end of</span>
  </p>
  <p class="small">
    <strong>Three things the chart says that the prose does not.</strong> The Hertfordshire family and
    the Berlin family are <em>contemporaries</em> — Henry Robert Wear and Jacob Leibholz were born five
    years apart, and their sons were in the same city by 1940. <strong>Everything converges on
    1948</strong>: Ivone marries twice in four months, Kurt files against her within the year, and
    Leonard is thirty-one and married to somebody else. And the right-hand lane keeps going —
    <strong>Gillian dies in March 2024</strong>, five months before this archive began looking for her.
  </p>

  <h2>The finding this archive cannot avoid</h2>
  <div class="panel wide">
    <h3>Kurt Israel Luwinski is very probably not Derrick's biological father <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></h3>
    <p>If he were, Derrick's DNA would carry about a quarter Ashkenazi Jewish from Jacob Leibholz and
    about a quarter East Prussian German from Auguste. It carries <strong>0.0%</strong> and
    <strong>1.4%</strong>. His nearest matches are British and Portuguese, and there is no German or
    Jewish cluster anywhere near the top of a list 5,464 long.</p>
    <p><strong>And the Y-DNA now agrees.</strong> Derrick's paternal haplogroup, read on 14 September
    2026, is <strong>R-S11477</strong> — R1b, branch <strong>U106/Z381</strong>, the North Sea lineage
    that peaks in the Netherlands and England. It is not a lineage characteristic of Ashkenazi paternal
    descent. Two independent systems, the same answer, and they agree on direction as well: the
    autosomal cluster is British, and the haplogroup's largest European population is England.</p>
    <p class="small"><a href={base + '/dna/'}>The evidence, the arithmetic, what the Y can and cannot prove →</a></p>
  </div>
  <p class="wide">
    That does not make the Berlin history untrue, and it does not make it less his. Jacob Leibholz died
    at Auschwitz; his brothers Samuel and Oskar died there too, and Georg at Chełmno. Kurt escaped in
    1933 and carried the name. It is the family Derrick was born into and named for. Whether it is also
    the family he descends from is a different question, and this archive keeps the two apart rather
    than pretending they are one.
  </p>


